## Returns the testing anti-patterns reference as a string. Assign and inject with ! at the call site.
pub fn anti_patterns(): String {
    return '''
    # Testing Anti-Patterns Reference

    Core principle: Test what the code does, not what the mocks do.
    Following strict TDD prevents all of these anti-patterns.

    ## Iron Laws
    1. NEVER test mock behaviour
    2. NEVER add test-only methods to production classes
    3. NEVER mock without understanding the dependency

    ## Anti-Pattern 1: Testing Mock Behaviour
    Asserting that a mock element exists proves only that the mock works.
    Gate: Before asserting on any mock — ask "Am I testing real behaviour or just mock existence?"
    If testing mock existence: delete the assertion or unmock the component.

    ## Anti-Pattern 2: Test-Only Methods in Production
    Methods only called by tests pollute production classes with dangerous cleanup code.
    Gate: Before adding any method — ask "Is this only used by tests?"
    If yes: put it in test utilities, not the production class.

    ## Anti-Pattern 3: Mocking Without Understanding
    Over-mocking breaks tests by removing side effects the test actually depends on.
    Gate: Before mocking anything — list every side effect the real method has.
    Ask: does this test depend on any of those? If unsure, run with real implementation first.

    ## Anti-Pattern 4: Incomplete Mocks
    Partial mocks hide structural assumptions. Downstream code silently depends on fields you omitted.
    Rule: Mock the COMPLETE data structure as it exists in reality, not just the fields you currently know about.

    ## Anti-Pattern 5: Tests as Afterthought
    Testing is part of implementation. Claiming complete without tests is false.
    TDD cycle: write failing test then implement to pass then refactor then claim complete.

    ## Red Flags
    - Assertion checks for *-mock test IDs
    - Methods only called in test files
    - Mock setup is more than 50% of the test
    - Test fails when you remove the mock
    - Mocking just to be safe
    - Cannot explain why the mock is needed
    '''
}
